# verify skill — adapter for a Spring Boot + MyBatis service.
#
# A worked example, generalized from a real production service. Every comment
# marked TRAP cost someone an hour to find. See spring-mybatis.md.
#
# Copy it, change the values, keep the reasoning.

# ==========================================================================
# Gate 1 — build
#
# TRAP: buildDir is redirected on purpose. An IDE with the project open writes
# into build/classes continuously. Gradle snapshots the task outputs, a file
# disappears mid-snapshot, and the build fails with:
#   Cannot access output property 'destinationDirectory' of task ':compileJava'
#   > java.nio.file.NoSuchFileException: build/classes/.../SomeDto__Javadoc.json
# That reads as a compile error in your diff and is not one. Adding `clean`
# does not help, because the IDE writes again immediately.
#
# A private build directory also stops gate 1 deleting build/libs/*.jar, which
# gate 4a needs to start the service. Gate 1 must not destroy what gate 4 needs.
# Add build-verify/ to .gitignore.
# ==========================================================================

VERIFY_BUILD_CMD="./gradlew compileJava --offline -Dorg.gradle.project.buildDir=build-verify"

# ==========================================================================
# Gate 2 — static checks
#
# TRAP: MyBatis mapper XML is invisible to the Java compiler. A malformed
# statement, an unescaped < in a comparison, or a typo in a statement id
# compiles clean and throws on the first call. An unescaped <> in mapper XML
# has crash-looped a production pod. xmllint is a cheap check for an
# expensive failure.
#
# xmllint catches malformed XML only. A valid-XML SQL error, a wrong column,
# or a join that returns too many rows belongs to gate 4 — and for a changed
# query, the value assertion in the happy variant is what tests it.
# ==========================================================================

VERIFY_STATIC_CMDS="
test-compile|./gradlew compileTestJava --offline -Dorg.gradle.project.buildDir=build-verify
mapper-xml|find src/main/resources -name '*Mapper.xml' -print0 | xargs -0 -n1 xmllint --noout && echo 'all mapper XML parses'
"

# ==========================================================================
# Gate 4a — the running service
#
# TRAP: the application port and the management port are usually different,
# and only one of them carries the context path. Probing health on the
# management port proves a different listener is alive. Probe health on the
# same base URL the scenario calls use.
#
# A second instance needs BOTH ports moved, not just the application port.
# ==========================================================================

VERIFY_BASE_URL="http://localhost:8080"

# The load-balancer probe, on the application port and behind the context
# path, and excluded from the security filter chain so it needs no token.
VERIFY_HEALTH_PATH="/api/health"

VERIFY_HEALTH_TIMEOUT=180

# Start from the built jar, not bootRun, so the process is the artifact.
# TRAP: a log config that writes to an absolute path fails to boot unless
# LOG_DIR is set, and the error names an appender, not the directory.
# TRAP: encrypted config values need their decryption password supplied, or
# boot dies on "Could not resolve placeholder".
VERIFY_RUN_CMD='export LOG_DIR="$(pwd)/logs-verify"; mkdir -p "$LOG_DIR"; nohup java -Dspring.profiles.active=dev -DLOG_DIR="$LOG_DIR" -Djasypt.encryptor.password="$CONFIG_DECRYPT_PASSWORD" -jar build/libs/app.jar > /tmp/app-verify.log 2>&1 &'

# Boot completion lands in the log file, not on stdout.
VERIFY_RUN_READY_CMD='grep -E "Started .*Application|Application run failed" "$(pwd)/logs-verify/app.log"'

# ==========================================================================
# Gate 4b — token
#
# TRAP: the bootstrap is often circular. A dev-only mint endpoint such as
# GET /api/test/generate-jwt is useless if its path is absent from the
# security config's permit list — the filter chain refuses the request with
# 403, so the endpoint that mints tokens needs a token.
#
# Check for this every time. The fix is usually an offline signing script that
# uses the same HS256 secret the dev profile validates with.
#
# TRAP: a service may also require a session in a cache alongside the JWT. Look
# for a "token only" flag in the auth filter's config. Without it the filter
# rejects a valid signature and the status looks like an application defect.
# ==========================================================================

VERIFY_TOKEN_MODE="command"

VERIFY_TOKEN_COMMAND='bash "$VERIFY_TARGET_DIR/scripts/generate-jwt.sh" "${APP_USER_ID:?set APP_USER_ID}" "${APP_USER_ROLE:-USER}" 2>/dev/null | tail -1'

# The script prints a bare token, so there is nothing to extract.
VERIFY_TOKEN_JQ=""

VERIFY_TOKEN_HEADER_NAME="Authorization"
VERIFY_TOKEN_HEADER_PREFIX="Bearer "

VERIFY_TOKEN_TTL=1500

# Every value that changes the identity. Without this a token for one role is
# reused for another, every authorization rule fails, and the receipts look
# like an application bug.
VERIFY_TOKEN_SUBJECT="${APP_USER_ID:-none}/${APP_USER_ROLE:-USER}"

# TRAP: an offline-minted token may carry fewer claims than the real login
# issues. Compare the signing helper's claim list against the production token
# factory. Endpoints scoped by a missing claim cannot be verified this way —
# that is a coverage gap to name in the report, not something to work around.

# ==========================================================================
# Gate 4c — accounts
# ==========================================================================

VERIFY_ACCOUNT_HOWTO="A minted token needs a subject that exists.
Use the project's own seeding path, not a hand-built row:
  ./gradlew flywayMigrate -Pseed=dev      # or the seed script, or the fixture
A synthesized account that skips a required relation yields a token that
authenticates and then fails every business rule, which reads as an
application bug and costs an hour.
Note which rows are created by seeding and which appear only on first login."

# ==========================================================================
# Calls
# ==========================================================================

VERIFY_CALL_TIMEOUT=30

# Extra redaction for identifiers that must not reach a receipt. These are sed
# expressions, newline separated.
VERIFY_REDACT_EXTRA=''

# ==========================================================================
# Gate 5 — report
# ==========================================================================

VERIFY_GLOSSARY_PATHS="CONTEXT.md
docs/adr/
GLOSSARY.md"

VERIFY_REPORT_LANG="en"

# ==========================================================================
# WARNING
#
# TRAP: a "local" run is often not local. A dev profile that points at a
# shared development database means a local JVM against remote shared data.
# A write variant changes data other people are using, and a VPN outage makes
# gate 4 BLOCKED rather than FAIL.
# ==========================================================================

VERIFY_WRITE_WARNING="Confirm the dev profile's datasource before any write variant. A shared dev database makes every write visible to the whole team."

# Hosts this skill must never call. Enforced, not advisory: scripts/lib.sh
# vf_guard_target refuses the request before curl runs, in call.sh, token.sh,
# and the health poll. Put your production hostnames here, as one extended
# regex. The mistake it prevents is the one a later verdict cannot undo.
#
# The environment ladder itself is in SKILL.md phase 0, and the target repo's
# own written rules win over both.
VERIFY_FORBIDDEN_HOSTS="^(www|api)\.example\.com$"
